Q: C++ : Recursion with a vector A: <p>This works,</p>

<pre><code>int listar(int v[],int index);

int main(){ 
    int vector[5]={1,2,3,4,5}; 
    listar(vector,(sizeof(vector)/sizeof(*vector))-1); 
    system("pause");
    return 0;
} 

int listar(int v[],int index)
{
    if(index&lt;0) return -1;
    cout&lt;&lt;v[index]&lt;&lt;"\n";
    listar(v,index-1);
    return v[index];
}
</code></pre>

<p>Note: You <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/204476/what-should-main-return-in-c-and-c">should</a> always use int main as the signature of main function.</p>

Q: How can I pass an immediate array to a C function accepting pointer? A: <p>You can write a wrapper as for example</p>
<pre><code>int func_1_call( auto...values ) 
    requires (sizeof...( values ) != 0 ) &amp;&amp;
    ( std::is_same_v&lt;decltype( values ), int&gt; &amp;&amp; ... ) 
{
    const int a[] = {values...};

    return func_1( static_cast&lt;int&gt;( std::size( a ) ), a );
}
</code></pre>
<p>or like</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename ...T&gt;
int func_1_call( T...values ) 
    requires (sizeof...( values ) != 0 ) &amp;&amp;
    ( std::is_same_v&lt;T, int&gt; &amp;&amp; ... ) 
{
    const T a[] = {values...};

    return func_1( static_cast&lt;int&gt;( std::size( a ) ), a );
}
</code></pre>
<p>and call it like</p>
<pre><code>func_1_call( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 );
</code></pre>
<p>As for your demonstration code then it is better to use <strong><code>std::array</code></strong> instead of <strong><code>std::vector</code></strong> provided that the passed sequence of values that represents an array is not very big because the last can allocate memory dynamically.</p>
<p>Also you may use the argument expression</p>
<pre><code>std::array{var_2, var_3}.data()
</code></pre>
<p>instead of</p>
<pre><code>std::array&lt;int, 2&gt;{var_2, var_3}.data()
</code></pre>
<p>if your compiler supports an appropriate version of the C++ Standard.</p>
